<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Lower Canada</h1><p class="st-meta">1791 CE – 1841 CE · capital Quebec</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French-speaking British colony on the lower St Lawrence whose clashes between its elected assembly and appointed rulers led to the rebellions of 1837 and 1838.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-act-1791">Constitutional Act</a> of 1791 divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-quebec">Province of Quebec</a> in two. The eastern part, along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec City</a> as capital, became Lower <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a>, where the great majority of people were French-speaking Catholics living under French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> and the seigneurial system.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lower <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> had an elected assembly, but real power lay with the governor and appointed councils dominated by an English-speaking merchant elite, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-clique">Château Clique</a>. The Parti canadien, later the Parti patriote under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-papineau">Louis-Joseph Papineau</a>, controlled the assembly and demanded control of revenues. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal's</a> merchants dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and wheat trades, while the Catholic Church held great influence over rural life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain's</a> refusal of reform led to armed rebellion in 1837 and 1838, crushed by troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lambton-1st-earl-of-durham">Lord Durham's</a> report recommended union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-canada">Upper Canada</a> to assimilate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-union-1840">Act of Union</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Province of Canada</a> in 1841.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lower-canada.jpg" alt="Lower Canada" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1791 CE – 1841 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quebec</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lord Dorchester, James Henry Craig, Lord Durham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of Lower Canada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=lower-canada" aria-label="Lower Canada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3099 438 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lower Canada lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3419 694L3377 637M3419 694L4997 651M3419 694L4997 651M3419 694L2907 423"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3377 637h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M2907 423h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3419 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3419 694h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=lower-canada">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
